            <ArticleTitle>Length of time spent working on a commercial construction site and the associations with worker characteristics.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>964-73</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/ajim.22461</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Construction workers move frequently from jobsite to jobsite, yet little is documented about length of stay on-site and associations with worker characteristics.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Using cross-sectional data, we investigated associations between worker characteristics (including trade and musculoskeletal pain) and length of stay on-site (dichotomized as &lt; 1 month, n = 554, and ≥ 1 month, n = 435).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Approximately, 56% of workers remained on the worksite for at least 1 month. Length of stay was significantly associated with workers' race/ethnicity, union status, title, trade, and musculoskeletal pain (P-values &lt; 0.05). Trades associated with longer length of stay included pipefitters and plumbers. Trades associated with shorter length of stay included operators and piledrivers. Workers with single-location pain had 2.21 times (95%CI: 1.52, 3.19) the odds of being short-term versus long-term, adjusting for trade, title, and race/ethnicity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The length of stay and associated characteristics provide important insight into how workers come and go on construction sites and the methodological challenges associated with traditional intervention evaluations.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
